B. Data Processing
Fig. 2. Sample accelerometer and gyroscope data captured from smartwatches worn by a healthy control (HC) participant and a Parkinson’s disease (PD) patient during motor task execution.
Firstly, the raw time-series data from the IMU sensor underwent preprocessing by eliminating noise from smartwatch’s vibration motor signaling the start of each task, the first second of each recording was removed. As the study focuses solely on classifying Parkinson’s Disease (PD) versus Healthy Controls (HC), individuals with Differential Diagnoses (DD) were excluded from the analysis to establish a clear baseline for a screening tool and avoid the complexities of a multi-class classification problem. Given the class imbalance between the PD and HC cohorts, an up-sampling was implemented to make the dataset balanced for training. Samples from the minority classes were randomly duplicated to make sure that each class had an equal number of individuals. This prevents the classification models to have a bias towards the majority class. Figure 2 shows the difference between a sample of the accelerometer and gyroscope data of a Healthy Control (HC) individual and a Parkinson’s Disease (PD) patient. The process of feature extraction varied according to the modeling approach. Statistical features were computed for conventional machine learning models, whereas raw multivariate time-series inputs were preserved for deep learning architectures such as CNN and LSTM.
• For Traditional ML Models: Statistical features were extracted from the IMU sensors, including data from both the accelerometer and gyroscope. The extracted features include mean, standard deviation, variance, minimum, maximum, and median.
• For Deep Neural Networks (DNN): For models such as CNN and LSTM, the raw sensor data was used directly without feature extraction. The time-series data from all sensor axes were stacked together to create a multichannel input. This allowed the models to automatically learn patterns for classifying PD.
C. Classification Models
To evaluate the most effective model, we implemented four architectures, categorized as either traditional machine learning model or deep learning model. Support Vector Machine (SVM) and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) are traditional models, while deep learning models included Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and hybrid CNN-LSTM model. We implemented both traditional machine learning models and deep learning models to compare performance in classifying Parkinson’s disease using smartwatch IMU data. By incorporating both traditional and deep learning models, we leverage the strengths of each approach to assess which is more suitable for accurate and efficient Parkinson’s Disease screening. Traditional machine learning models were utilized to establish a baseline for Parkinson’s Disease (PD) classification based on statistical features extracted from the smartwatch IMU data. These features, computed from acceleration and angular velocity signals, included common time-domain indicators that represent the overall motion characteristics of each participant during specific tasks. Support Vector Machine (SVM) and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) models are used due to their proven effectiveness in biomedical signal classification tasks. SVM is known for its strong performance in high-dimensional feature spaces and its ability to handle small- to medium-sized datasets effectively, making it wellsuited for our structured IMU-derived feature set. For instance, studies have shown that machine learning models like SVM can effectively train on IMU-derived statistical features to accurately evaluate gait impairment in patients with Parkinson’s disease [16]. XGBoost, a powerful ensemble learning method based on decision trees, is highly robust against overfitting and excels at capturing complex, nonlinear patterns in the data [17]. Hyperparameters for both models were optimized using grid search to ensure optimal classification performance. By incorporating these two traditional approaches, we aimed to assess how well interpretable, lightweight models perform compared to deep learning architectures, enabling insights into the trade-offs between complexity, performance, and practicality in real-world PD screening scenarios. For deep learning models, raw data from the IMU were stacked to form a multiple-channel input. Each sample had the shape of (N,12), where N is the number of time steps.
