The 12 channels represent the 3 accelerometer and 3 gyroscope axes from both wrists [18]. We implemented two deep learning architectures: a 1D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and a hybrid CNN-LSTM model. The CNN was chosen for its ability to effectively extract local spatial features and movement patterns from sequential sensor data through convolutional filters. This makes it well-suited for identifying subtle movement irregularities associated with Parkinson’s Disease. To complement this, we employed a CNN-LSTM model, where the CNN component first extracts high-level spatial features from the IMU signal, which are then passed to a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) layer. The LSTM captures temporal dependencies and sequential dynamics over time, which are crucial for modeling motion characteristics in PD. Other studies, such as Choi et al. [19], have also demonstrated the effectiveness of using CNN with time series sensor data from the 6-minute walk test, to detect Parkinson’s disease. • Convolutional Neural Network (CNN): We utilized 1D CNN, which is effective at identifying local patterns and features within sequential data. Our architecture consists of the following layer: – An Input Layer accepting samples of shape (N, 12) – A series of two 1D Convolutional Blocks containing Conv1D, ReLU, and MaxPooling1D layers – A Dropout layer for regularization to prevent overfitting – An Adaptive Pooling 1D Layer – Flatten Layer to convert 2D features into 1D vector – Two Fully Connected (FC) Layers, which use the extracted features to perform the final classification with dropout for regularization – An Output Layer for classification • Hybrid Convolutional Neural Network–Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-LSTM): This architecture was designed to combine the strengths of both CNN and LSTM networks. The CNN component extracts spatial features from the IMU data, and the LSTM component learns the sequence of these patterns over time. We used the same
1D Convolutional Blocks used in the CNN model. The CNN component extracts a sequence of feature vectors, which is then fed into LSTM Layer. After that, the output from the LSTM is passed to an FC Layer and an Output Layer for binary classification.
III. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
We evaluated several machine learning models, including traditional approaches such as SVM and XGBoost, as well as deep learning models like CNN and CNN-LSTM, across 11 assessments. The performance was measured using accuracy and F1-Score, a metric representing harmonic mean of precision and recall, making it useful for assessing model’s effectiveness. It is calculated using the following formula:
F1 = 2 · Precision · Recall
