As shown in Table II, certain individual tasks achieved high classification accuracy, indicating that comprehensive multitask assessments may not be required for effective Parkinson’s Disease screening. Multiple upsampling methods such as SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) [20] and ADASYN (Adaptive Synthetic Algorithm) [21] have been explored in our experiments. Our initial experiments suggested that the more complex data generation approach resulted in slightly worse performance than the simpler naive upsampling method. This indicated that synthetic samples might not have accurately captured patterns of Parkinson’s disease. Traditional machine learning models, particularly XGBoost, also demonstrated robust performance. For instance, the ”Finger-to-Nose” task, XGBoost achieved an accuracy of 84.51%, whereas more complex models like CNN and CNN-LSTM had lower accuracies of 73.24% and 67.61%, respectively. XGBoost also achieved high accuracies in other tasks, such as ”Finger-to-Target” (81.69%), ”Cross and Extend Arms” (81.69%), ”Finger-to-Nose” (84.51%), and ”Entrainment” (80.28%). This suggests that traditional models can be effective for this type of data as well as more complex deep learning models.
