Deep learning models, while not consistently outperforming traditional approaches, demonstrated strong potential in specific tasks. In particular, the CNN model outperformed both XGBoost and SVM on the ”Cross and Extend Arms” and ”Finger-to-Finger” tasks, achieving accuracies of 84.51% and 87.32%, respectively. The accuracy on the ”Finger-to-Finger” task was the highest across all models and tasks evaluated. These results highlight the capability of deep learning methods to automatically extract meaningful features from raw IMU sensor data, making them highly effective for Parkinson’s Disease classification in targeted scenarios. Considering both classification accuracy and ease of performance, the ”Finger-to-Finger” task emerges as a strong candidate for practical Parkinson’s Disease (PD) screening. It achieved the highest accuracy (87.32%) while requiring only a simple and intuitive hand motion that is easy to perform without supervision. The ”Relaxed Task” also demonstrated promising accuracy (80.28%), making it another viable option for at-home screening, especially for individuals with limited motor ability. In contrast, more complex tasks such as ”Entrainment,” although accurate (80.28%), demand greater physical coordination and may not be suitable for elderly individuals or those with mobility limitations. Simplest tasks like ”Resting” are easier but yield lower predictive performance (74.65%). Therefore, selecting tasks that are both diagnostically effective and user-friendly is crucial for realworld deployment. The results suggest that a single assessment task may be sufficient as a fast and accessible screening tool for Parkinson’s Disease. This approach could benefit patients by enabling simpler, more convenient, and scalable early diagnosis, where individuals can perform a preliminary screening at home and use the results to decide whether to seek further diagnosis from a neurologist.
IV. CONCLUSION
This study investigated the feasibility of using smartwatchbased IMU data from individual tasks to accurately classify individuals with Parkinson’s disease against healthy controls. Traditional machine learning models, particularly SVM and XGBoost, performed consistently well across most motor tasks. Notably, the deep learning-based CNN model achieved the highest classification accuracy of 87.32% on the ”Fingerto-Finger” task. These findings support the development of an efficient, low-cost Parkinson’s Disease screening method using consumer-grade smartwatches. By showing that high diagnostic performance can be obtained from a single, simple motor task, our approach enables accessible, at-home screening—facilitating earlier detection and timely intervention for individuals at risk of Parkinson’s Disease. Future work should focus on implementing these machine learning models on larger and more diverse datasets to ensure that the model generalizes well across different population demographics and a wide range of smartwatch devices that differ in hardware configurations and sensor sampling parameters. This could further enhance the classification performance.
Moreover, exploring other features that will contribute to the classification in the traditional ML models could provide greater accuracy and a deeper understanding of movement patterns that are characteristic of Parkinson’s disease. Finally, investigating other types of model architecture, such as attention-based models, could be a promising direction for future research, as they may improve the classification accuracy and lead to more reliable Parkinson’s disease screening.
